  "live_records": {
    "q-39400": {
      "dom_id": "step-q39400",
      "name": "Question 1",
      "problem": "Given the orthogonal basis $B$ of ${R}^{3}$ and the vector $x$ below, find the value of $29{x}_{1}$, where ${x}_{1}$ is the first component of $[x]_{B}.$\n\n$$\nB=\\{[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-18 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -17\\end{aligned}]\\},\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$",
      "worked_solution": "Let's denote the vectors of $B$ as ${q}_{1},\\,{q}_{2},\\,{q}_{3}.$ \n\nSince $B$ is an orthogonal basis, we have that $[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]$, where ${x}_{i}=\\frac{x\\cdot {q}_{i}}{{q}_{i}\\cdot {q}_{i}}.$ Computing ${x}_{1},$ we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =\\frac{x\\cdot {q}_{1}}{{q}_{1}\\cdot {q}_{1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\cdot 3+(-1)\\cdot 4+2\\cdot (-2)}{{3}^{2}+{4}^{2}+(-2)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{15-4-4}{9+16+4} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{29}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n29{x}_{1}=29\\cdot \\frac{7}{29}=7.\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
