    "q-81138": {
      "dom_id": "step-q81138",
      "name": "Question 2",
      "problem": "Given the orthogonal basis $B$ of ${R}^{3}$ and the vector $x$ below, find the value of $20{x}_{2}$, where ${x}_{2}$ is the second component of $[x]_{B}.$\n\n$$\nB=\\{[\\begin{aligned}10 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 25 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\\},\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]\n$$",
      "worked_solution": "Let's denote the vectors of $B$ as ${q}_{1},\\,{q}_{2},\\,{q}_{3}.$ \n\nSince $B$ is an orthogonal basis, we have that $[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]$, where ${x}_{i}=\\frac{x\\cdot {q}_{i}}{{q}_{i}\\cdot {q}_{i}}.$ Computing ${x}_{2},$ we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =\\frac{x\\cdot {q}_{2}}{{q}_{2}\\cdot {q}_{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\cdot 1+4\\cdot 0+(-2)\\cdot (-2)}{{1}^{2}+{0}^{2}+(-2)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3+4}{1+0+4} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n20{x}_{2}=20\\cdot \\frac{7}{5}=28.\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
