    "q-40291": {
      "dom_id": "step-q40291",
      "name": "Question 4",
      "problem": "Given the orthogonal basis $B$ of ${R}^{2}$ and the vector $x$ below, find the value of $26{x}_{1},$ where ${x}_{1}$ is the first component of $[x]_{B}.$\n\n$$\nB=\\{[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-18 \\\\ 27\\end{aligned}]\\},\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$",
      "worked_solution": "Let's denote the vectors of $B$ as ${q}_{1},\\,{q}_{2}.$ \n\nSince $B$ is an orthogonal basis, we have that $[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]$, where ${x}_{i}=\\frac{x\\cdot {q}_{i}}{{q}_{i}\\cdot {q}_{i}}.$ Computing ${x}_{1},$ we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =\\frac{x\\cdot {q}_{1}}{{q}_{1}\\cdot {q}_{1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\cdot 3+(-1)\\cdot 2}{{3}^{2}+{2}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{9-2}{9+4} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{13}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n26{x}_{1}=26\\cdot \\frac{7}{13}=14.\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
