    "q-39491": {
      "dom_id": "step-q39491",
      "name": "Question 5",
      "problem": "Given the orthogonal basis $B$ of ${R}^{3}$ and the vector $x$ below, find the value of $3{x}_{2}$, where ${x}_{2}$ is the second component of $[x]_{B}.$\n\n$$\nB=\\{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\\},\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$",
      "worked_solution": "Let's denote the vectors of $B$ as ${q}_{1},\\,{q}_{2},\\,{q}_{3}.$ \n\nSince $B$ is an orthogonal basis, we have that $[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]$, where ${x}_{i}=\\frac{x\\cdot {q}_{i}}{{q}_{i}\\cdot {q}_{i}}.$ Computing ${x}_{2},$ we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =\\frac{x\\cdot {q}_{2}}{{q}_{2}\\cdot {q}_{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\cdot 1+(-4)\\cdot 2+2\\cdot 1}{{1}^{2}+{2}^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2-8+2}{1+4+1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n3{x}_{2}=3\\cdot (-\\frac{2}{3})=-2.\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
