      "name": "Question 10",
      "problem": "Given the **orthonormal** basis $B=\\{[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]\\}$ of ${R}^{2},$ find $[x]_{B}$ if $x=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$",
      "worked_solution": "Let's denote the vectors of $B$ as ${q}_{1},\\,{q}_{2}.$ \n\nSince $B$ is an orthonormal basis, we have that $[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]$, where ${x}_{i}=x\\cdot {q}_{i}.$ Computing the components of $x,$ we get the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]=\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{2}}-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{2}}=\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ {x}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]=\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{2}}+\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{2}}=\\frac{6}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have \n\n$$\n[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{6}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
